text:
YAML anatomy
Every file has this shape:
network:
  version: 2
  renderer: networkd
  ethernets: {}
  bonds: {}
  vlans: {}
  bridges: {}
  vrfs: {}
  tunnels: {}
Device IDs (eth0, bond0, br-lab) are Netplan names. If the ID equals the kernel interface name, you do not need match:. If you want to select by MAC, driver or a glob, use match: and, if you also want a stable name, set-name:.
Members of a bond or a bridge, and the parent of a VLAN (link:), must themselves be defined in the same merged configuration. A VLAN ID is 0–4094. VRF tables are first-class (vrfs:, compulsory table:); they are the same idea as Linux policy routing and VRF, covered later in the Layer 3 part.

Verification checklist
ip -br a
ip route
ip -6 route
ip route get &lt;dst&gt;
ping -c 2 &lt;dst&gt;
ping -6 -c 2 &lt;dst6&gt;

Asymmetric routing
Forward path R1→R2→R3, return R3→R4→R1. Stateless ping may work; stateful firewalls and some reverse-path filters break.
Always verify both directions.
# from each side
ping -c 2 &lt;other&gt;
traceroute -n &lt;other&gt;

IPv6 notes
IPv6 routers do not fragment in the same way; PMTUD is even more critical. Minimum link MTU is 1280.
ping -6 -M do -s 1400 &lt;dst&gt;
ip -6 route get &lt;dst&gt;

Production Configuration Patterns (not run here)

ISC DHCP Server (dhcpd.conf)
The following snippet illustrates conditional architecture matching and iPXE chainloading in ISC dhcpd. ISC ended maintenance of ISC DHCP in 2022 and points users to Kea, but dhcpd.conf files like this are still common:
# (not run here)
option space ipxe;
option ipxe-encap-settings code 175 = encapsulate ipxe;
option client-arch code 93 = unsigned integer 16;

subnet 10.66.0.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 {
  range 10.66.0.100 10.66.0.200;
  next-server 10.66.0.1;

  if exists user-class and option user-class = "iPXE" {
      filename "http://10.66.0.1/boot.ipxe";
  } else if option client-arch = 00:07 or option client-arch = 00:09 {
      filename "ipxe.efi";
  } else if option client-arch = 00:00 {
      filename "undionly.kpxe";
  } else if option client-arch = 00:10 {
      option vendor-class-identifier "HTTPClient";
      filename "http://10.66.0.1/ipxe.efi";
  }
}


dnsmasq Combined DHCP/TFTP Server
dnsmasq provides built-in DHCP and TFTP services with concise tag matching:
# (not run here)
interface=eth0
dhcp-range=10.66.0.100,10.66.0.200,255.255.255.0,12h

# Match architecture options
dhcp-match=set:bios,option:client-arch,0
dhcp-match=set:efi64,option:client-arch,7
dhcp-match=set:efi64,option:client-arch,9
dhcp-match=set:httpboot,option:client-arch,16
dhcp-userclass=set:ipxe,iPXE

# Boot file assignment
dhcp-boot=tag:bios,tag:!ipxe,undionly.kpxe,10.66.0.1
dhcp-boot=tag:efi64,tag:!ipxe,ipxe.efi,10.66.0.1
dhcp-boot=tag:httpboot,tag:!ipxe,http://10.66.0.1/ipxe.efi
dhcp-option-force=tag:httpboot,60,HTTPClient
dhcp-boot=tag:ipxe,http://10.66.0.1/boot.ipxe

# Internal TFTP Server Configuration
enable-tftp
tftp-root=/srv/tftp


Kea DHCP Server (kea-dhcp4.conf)
ISC Kea uses JSON evaluation rules for client class matching:
// (not run here)
{
  "Dhcp4": {
    "interfaces-config": { "interfaces": [ "eth0" ] },
    "lease-database": { "type": "memfile" },
    "client-classes": [
      {
        "name": "ipxe",
        "test": "option[77].hex == 'iPXE'",
        "boot-file-name": "http://10.66.0.1/boot.ipxe"
      },
      {
        "name": "uefi-x64",
        "test": "option[93].hex == 0x0007 and not (option[77].hex == 'iPXE')",
        "next-server": "10.66.0.1",
        "boot-file-name": "ipxe.efi"
      },
      {
        "name": "bios-x86",
        "test": "option[93].hex == 0x0000 and not (option[77].hex == 'iPXE')",
        "next-server": "10.66.0.1",
        "boot-file-name": "undionly.kpxe"
      }
    ],
    "subnet4": [
      {
        "subnet": "10.66.0.0/24",
        "pools": [ { "pool": "10.66.0.100 - 10.66.0.200" } ]
      }
    ]
  }
}

Server behaviour and Kea DHCPv4
Under RFC 3046 section 2.2, a DHCP server that understands option 82 SHALL echo the entire option 82—verbatim, every sub-option intact—in all replies (DHCPOFFER, DHCPACK, DHCPNAK), and SHOULD place it as the last option before End (255). Servers that do not understand option 82 simply ignore it, which breaks relays that rely on the echoed circuit-id to know which access port or VLAN to send the unicast/broadcast reply down.
Section 2.2 also states an important rule for renewals: servers SHOULD be prepared to handle option 82 in unicast messages, but MUST NOT expect it to always be there, because RFC 3046 does not require relays to see unicast traffic from clients. And if a server cannot fit the full option into a reply, it SHALL send the reply without it and SHOULD count the event.
Modern DHCPv4 servers like ISC Kea expose option 82 sub-options directly in subnet selection, host reservations, and client classification. According to the Kea ARM (“The DHCPv4 Server”), Kea understands these RAI sub-options natively:

circuit-id (1): Used for host reservations when circuit-id is listed in host-reservation-identifiers.
remote-id (2) and subscriber-id (6): Usable for reservations and identification via the flex-id hook library.
link-selection (5): Overrides giaddr for subnet selection (unless disabled with the compatibility knob ignore-rai-link-selection).
server-id-override (11): Accepted as the Server Identifier (option 54) in replies and matched on incoming DHCPREQUEST packets.
relay-id (12) and relay-port (19): Parsed and tracked for relay identification and non-standard relay ports.

For any sub-option—including vendor-specific ones or raw byte matches—Kea’s client classification expression language exposes relay4[CODE].hex. For example, to classify packets whose remote-id (sub-option 2) equals 0x01020304 and pin a reservation by circuit-id, your Kea configuration looks like this (from the Kea ARM, not run):
{
  "Dhcp4": {
    "host-reservation-identifiers": [ "circuit-id", "hw-address" ],
    "client-classes": [
      {
        "name": "matched-remote",
        "test": "relay4[2].hex == 0x01020304"
      }
    ],
    "subnet4": [
      {
        "id": 1,
        "subnet": "10.20.30.0/24",
        "pools": [ { "pool": "10.20.30.100 - 10.20.30.199" } ],
        "reservations": [
          {
            "circuit-id": "'sw-acc-07 eth1/0/7:120'",
            "ip-address": "10.20.30.77"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
Notice the ordering in host-reservation-identifiers: putting "circuit-id" before "hw-address" tells Kea to check for a circuit-id match first and fall back to MAC-based reservations only if no circuit-id reservation matches.

Production Deployments and Server Configuration (not run here)
Production DNS deployments utilize dedicated DNS daemons such as BIND 9 or Knot DNS to handle zone transfers, NOTIFY signals, and cryptographic keys.

Key Generation
Before configuring daemons, generate a shared TSIG key using the standard utility tsig-keygen (not run here):
tsig-keygen -a hmac-sha256 xfr-key
This outputs a configuration block containing the secret string.


BIND 9 Configuration (named.conf)
On the primary server (IP 10.53.0.1), define the key and zone permissions (not run here):
key "xfr-key" {
    algorithm hmac-sha256;
    secret "c2VjcmV0LXNoYXJlZC1ieS10d28tc2VydmVycw==";
};

zone "lab.example" {
    type primary;
    file "/var/bind/db.lab.example";
    allow-transfer { key xfr-key; };
    also-notify { 10.53.0.2 key xfr-key; };
    notify yes;
};
On the secondary server (IP 10.53.0.2), configure the secondary zone stanza (not run here):
key "xfr-key" {
    algorithm hmac-sha256;
    secret "c2VjcmV0LXNoYXJlZC1ieS10d28tc2VydmVycw==";
};

zone "lab.example" {
    type secondary;
    primaries { 10.53.0.1 key xfr-key; };
    file "/var/cache/bind/db.lab.example";
};


Knot DNS Configuration (knot.conf)
Knot DNS uses YAML-based configuration syntax. The equivalent primary and secondary definitions are structured as follows (not run here):
key:
  - id: xfr-key
    algorithm: hmac-sha256
    secret: c2VjcmV0LXNoYXJlZC1ieS10d28tc2VydmVycw==

remote:
  - id: secondary-server
    address: 10.53.0.2
    key: xfr-key

acl:
  - id: xfr-acl
    action: transfer
    key: xfr-key

zone:
  - domain: lab.example
    notify: secondary-server
    acl: xfr-acl


Manual Testing with dig
Administrative testing of zone transfers can be performed using dig (not run here):
To request a full AXFR transfer using TSIG authentication:
dig @10.53.0.1 lab.example AXFR -y hmac-sha256:xfr-key:c2VjcmV0LXNoYXJlZC1ieS10d28tc2VydmVycw==
To request an IXFR incremental transfer specifying serial 2026111401:
dig @10.53.0.1 lab.example IXFR=2026111401 -y hmac-sha256:xfr-key:c2VjcmV0LXNoYXJlZC1ieS10d28tc2VydmVycw==
-y puts the secret on the command line, where ps and shell history can see it. Outside a lab, write the tsig-keygen output to a file and use dig -k xfr-key.conf instead.
The Knot snippet shows only the primary side. On a Knot secondary the same key is defined, the primary becomes a remote, and the zone gets master: &lt;remote-id&gt; (renamed primary in newer releases; check the documentation for your version) plus an acl that allows notify from that remote.

Production deployment configurations
Production implementations of resolver filtering and HTTP Host validation utilize dedicated daemon configurations.

Production dnsmasq setup (not run here)
# /etc/dnsmasq.conf
# Reject upstream answers in private IP ranges
stop-dns-rebind

# Allow loopback addresses in answers (for local ad-block lists)
rebind-localhost-ok

# Allow private IP answers for explicit local domain names
rebind-domain-ok=/home.arpa/


Production Unbound setup (not run here)
# /etc/unbound/unbound.conf
server:
    # Deny public domain answers pointing to private/loopback IPv4/IPv6 spaces
    private-address: 10.0.0.0/8
    private-address: 172.16.0.0/12
    private-address: 192.168.0.0/16
    private-address: 169.254.0.0/16
    private-address: fd00::/8
    private-address: fe80::/10
    private-address: ::ffff:0:0/96

    # Allow private IP answers for local infrastructure domains
    private-domain: "home.arpa"


Production NGINX host validation setup (not run here)
# /etc/nginx/sites-available/default
# Catch-all server block to drop requests with missing or untrusted Host headers
server {
    listen 80 default_server;
    listen [::]:80 default_server;
    server_name _;
    return 444;
}

# Explicit server block for authorized internal hostnames
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name admin.home.arpa 10.0.0.5;

    location / {
        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080;
    }
}

Daemon configuration snippets (not run here)

FRR Configuration
FRR supports TCP-MD5 and GTSM (ttl-security). TCP-AO is not supported in FRR: the feature request FRRouting/frr#7240 was still open when this chapter was written (2026-11-14). If you need TCP-AO on a Linux router today, BIRD (2.17+ or 3.1+) and ExaBGP 6 are the open-source options.
! (not run here)
router bgp 65001
 neighbor 10.1.0.2 remote-as 65002
 neighbor 10.1.0.2 password s3cret
 neighbor 10.1.0.2 ttl-security hops 1
Note: ttl-security hops 1 instructs FRR to configure IP_MINTTL=255 on the receiving socket and set outgoing packet TTL to 255. ttl-security and ebgp-multihop are mutually exclusive in FRR.


BIRD Configuration
BIRD supports TCP-MD5, GTSM, and TCP-AO (added in BIRD 2.17 / 3.1).

BIRD with TCP-MD5 and GTSM
# (not run here)
protocol bgp peer_md5 {
    local 10.1.0.1 as 65001;
    neighbor 10.1.0.2 as 65002;
    password "s3cret";
    ttl security yes;
}


BIRD with TCP-AO
# (not run here)
protocol bgp peer_ao {
    local 10.1.0.1 as 65001;
    neighbor 10.1.0.2 as 65002;
    authentication ao;
    keys {
        key {
            send id 1;
            recv id 1;
            secret "SuperSecretKey123";
            algorithm hmac sha256;
            preferred;
        };
    };
}



Router operating systems
Junos, Cisco IOS XR and Nokia SR OS implement TCP-MD5, GTSM and TCP-AO through their key-chain features. Syntax differs between releases, so take it from the vendor documentation for the release you run rather than from a book. Whatever the vendor, the interoperability checklist is the same: algorithm, MAC length, SendID/RecvID pairing (my SendID is your RecvID), and which key is preferred at session start.

A host firewall that loads atomically
nft -f file reads the whole file and commits it as one transaction. Either every line applies or none does, and there is never a moment with half a firewall. That is the main operational win over a shell script of iptables commands.
This file is for a server with SSH from a management network, and HTTP and HTTPS from anywhere:


host-firewall.nft

#!/usr/sbin/nft -f

# Recreate only our own table. Never "flush ruleset" on a host that
# also runs Docker, a container runtime, or a distro firewall:
# it deletes their tables too.
table inet host
delete table inet host

table inet host {
    set mgmt_v4 {
        type ipv4_addr
        flags interval
        elements = { 192.0.2.0/24, 198.51.100.7 }
    }

    set mgmt_v6 {
        type ipv6_addr
        flags interval
        elements = { 2001:db8:100::/56 }
    }

    set public_tcp {
        type inet_service
        elements = { 80, 443 }
    }

    chain input {
        type filter hook input priority filter; policy drop;

        ct state established,related accept
        ct state invalid drop
        iif lo accept

        # IPv4 diagnostics: allow, but rate-limit echo
        ip protocol icmp icmp type { destination-unreachable, time-exceeded, parameter-problem } accept
        ip protocol icmp icmp type echo-request limit rate 10/second accept

        # IPv6 does not work without these (RFC 4890)
        meta l4proto ipv6-icmp icmpv6 type { destination-unreachable, packet-too-big, time-exceeded, parameter-problem } accept
        meta l4proto ipv6-icmp icmpv6 type { nd-neighbor-solicit, nd-neighbor-advert, nd-router-advert } ip6 hoplimit 255 accept
        meta l4proto ipv6-icmp icmpv6 type echo-request limit rate 10/second accept

        ip saddr @mgmt_v4 tcp dport 22 ct state new counter accept
        ip6 saddr @mgmt_v6 tcp dport 22 ct state new counter accept

        tcp dport @public_tcp ct state new counter accept

        limit rate 5/minute log prefix "host-drop: " level info
        counter
    }

    chain forward {
        type filter hook forward priority filter; policy drop;
    }

    chain output {
        type filter hook output priority filter; policy accept;
    }
}

What each block is doing:

table inet host then delete table inet host. The first line creates the table if it is missing, so the delete never fails. The second removes it with everything inside. The block that follows recreates it. All three happen in one transaction. This is the safe replacement for flush ruleset.
inet family. One table, one set of chains, both IPv4 and IPv6. Rules that use ip saddr only match IPv4 packets; ip6 saddr only IPv6.
ct state established,related accept first. Most packets belong to existing flows. Accepting them first keeps the chain short for nearly every packet. related covers ICMP errors about an existing flow and helper-tracked connections.
ct state invalid drop. Packets conntrack cannot place, for example a TCP segment that is not part of any known connection and is not a SYN.
ICMPv6 neighbour discovery with hoplimit 255. ND messages must be sent with hop limit 255, so anything lower came from off-link. Dropping ND means the host cannot resolve its neighbours and IPv6 stops working; dropping packet-too-big breaks path MTU discovery.
limit rate 5/minute log ... before the policy drop. A log rule without a limit is a disk-filling denial-of-service you wrote yourself.
The final bare counter counts what the policy is about to drop. It costs nothing and answers “is anything hitting the drop?”.

Check it, load it, read it back:
nft -c -f host-firewall.nft        # parse and validate only
nft -f host-firewall.nft           # commit atomically
nft list table inet host
nft -a list chain inet host input  # -a shows rule handles
Illustrative, not captured: nft -a list chain prints each rule followed by # handle N. The handle is how you delete or insert next to a single rule:
table inet host {
    chain input {
        type filter hook input priority filter; policy drop;
        ct state established,related accept # handle 7
        ct state invalid drop # handle 8
        ...
nft delete rule inet host input handle 8
nft insert rule inet host input position 7 tcp dport 9100 ip saddr @mgmt_v4 accept
insert ... position N adds before handle N; add ... position N adds after it.

Sets, maps and concatenations
A list of twenty ip saddr X accept rules is evaluated one rule at a time. A set is one lookup, whatever its size. Use sets for anything that is a list.








Form
Example
Use




Anonymous set
tcp dport { 22, 80, 443 }
A fixed list inside one rule


Named set
set public_tcp { type inet_service; ... } and tcp dport @public_tcp
A list you change at runtime, or use in several rules


Interval set
flags interval with 192.0.2.0/24 or 1000-2000
Prefixes and ranges


Timeout set
flags timeout; timeout 10m
Entries that expire, such as temporary bans


Map
ip daddr map { 10.0.0.1 : 10.1.0.1 }
Look up a value, for example a NAT target


Verdict map
iifname vmap { "lan0" : jump from_lan, "wan0" : jump from_wan }
Dispatch to a chain by key in one lookup


Concatenation
ip saddr . tcp dport { 192.0.2.10 . 22, 192.0.2.11 . 5432 }
Match a tuple, not two independent lists



Change a named set without touching the rules:
nft add element inet host mgmt_v4 { 203.0.113.50 }
nft delete element inet host mgmt_v4 { 198.51.100.7 }
nft list set inet host mgmt_v4
The concatenation row matters for correctness, not just speed. ip saddr { A, B } tcp dport { 22, 5432 } allows A to 5432 and B to 22 as well. ip saddr . tcp dport { A . 22, B . 5432 } allows exactly the two pairs you meant.
A verdict map turns an interface-by-interface ruleset into a dispatcher. Each zone gets its own regular chain:


zones-fragment.nft

table inet edge {
    chain from_lan {
        accept
    }

    chain from_wan {
        tcp dport 22 drop
        return
    }

    chain forward {
        type filter hook forward priority filter; policy drop;
        ct state established,related accept
        iifname vmap { "lan0" : jump from_lan, "wan0" : jump from_wan }
    }
}

A packet from wan0 jumps to from_wan. If nothing there decides, return comes back to forward, which reaches its policy and drops.

Dynamic sets: per-source rate limits
A rule can add to a set as packets pass. This one tracks each source address that opens new SSH connections and drops a source once it exceeds three new connections a minute:


ssh-meter.nft

table inet host {
    set ssh_meter {
        type ipv4_addr
        flags dynamic, timeout
        timeout 5m
    }

    chain input {
        tcp dport 22 ct state new add @ssh_meter { ip saddr limit rate over 3/minute } drop
    }
}

The limit is kept per element, so one noisy source does not affect anyone else. If you load this fragment on top of the earlier file, it adds a second rule to the existing input chain, after the accepts. Place it before the SSH accept rules (insert or by editing the main file) or it will never be reached.

NAT on a gateway
NAT needs a nat-type chain on the right hook. Conntrack makes the decision on the first packet of a flow. Every later packet of that flow, in both directions, is translated from the conntrack entry without visiting the NAT chains again. That is why changing a NAT rule does not affect existing connections until they end.


gateway-nat.nft

table ip nat
delete table ip nat

table ip nat {
    chain prerouting {
        type nat hook prerouting priority dstnat; policy accept;
        iifname "wan0" tcp dport 8080 dnat to 10.0.0.10:80
    }

    chain postrouting {
        type nat hook postrouting priority srcnat; policy accept;
        oifname "wan0" ip saddr 10.0.0.0/24 masquerade
    }
}

table inet gwfilter
delete table inet gwfilter

table inet gwfilter {
    chain forward {
        type filter hook forward priority filter; policy drop;
        ct state established,related accept
        ct state invalid drop
        iifname "lan0" oifname "wan0" accept
        iifname "wan0" oifname "lan0" ct status dnat accept
    }
}


dnat to in prerouting rewrites the destination before routing, so the kernel routes the packet to 10.0.0.10.
masquerade in postrouting rewrites the source to the address of wan0 at the time the flow starts. Use snat to 203.0.113.5 instead when the WAN address is static: masquerade has to look up the interface address for each new flow and flushes its entries when the interface goes down.
NAT does not open the firewall. The forward chain must still allow the translated traffic. ct status dnat accepts only flows that were destination-NATed, so the WAN side cannot reach any other LAN host.
Forwarding must be on: sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1 (and persist it in /etc/sysctl.d/).

The ip family is used here for NAT because the example is IPv4 only. Recent kernels also support NAT in the inet family; with IPv6 you rarely want NAT at all.

The FlowSpec lab
flowspec.py implements the IPv4 encoding, a decoder that prints rules in a readable form, a matcher that applies the RFC’s semantics to a packet description, and the traffic-rate community encoding.


flowspec.py

#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""flowspec.py: RFC 8955 IPv4 Flow Specification NLRI encoder, decoder and matcher (stdlib only)."""
import ipaddress, struct

NAMES = {1: "dst", 2: "src", 3: "proto", 4: "port", 5: "dport", 6: "sport", 7: "icmp-type",
         8: "icmp-code", 9: "tcp-flags", 10: "length", 11: "dscp", 12: "fragment"}
BITMASK = {9, 12}
OPS = {"==": 0b001, "&gt;": 0b010, "&gt;=": 0b011, "&lt;": 0b100, "&lt;=": 0b101, "!=": 0b110}
FRAG = {"DF": 1, "IsF": 2, "FF": 4, "LF": 8}

def enc_prefix(t, net):
    net = ipaddress.ip_network(net)
    nbytes = (net.prefixlen + 7) // 8
    return bytes([t, net.prefixlen]) + net.network_address.packed[:nbytes]

def enc_list(t, terms):
    """terms: list of (and_bit, op, value). op is '==', '&gt;=', ... or for bitmasks 'match'/'any'/'!match'."""
    out = bytearray([t])
    for i, (and_bit, op, value) in enumerate(terms):
        size = 0 if value &lt; 256 else 1                     # 1- or 2-octet values
        b = (0x80 if i == len(terms) - 1 else 0) | (0x40 if and_bit and i else 0) | (size &lt;&lt; 4)
        if t in BITMASK:
            b |= {"any": 0, "match": 1, "!any": 2, "!match": 3}[op]
        else:
            b |= OPS[op]
        out += bytes([b]) + value.to_bytes(1 &lt;&lt; size, "big")
    return bytes(out)

def encode(components):
    """components: list of (type, arg) in increasing type order -&gt; NLRI with length prefix."""
    body = b"".join(enc_prefix(t, a) if t in (1, 2) else enc_list(t, a) for t, a in components)
    assert len(body) &lt; 240, "extended 2-octet length not needed for these examples"
    return bytes([len(body)]) + body

def decode(nlri):
    n, i, comps = nlri[0], 1, []
    end = 1 + n
    while i &lt; end:
        t = nlri[i]; i += 1
        if t in (1, 2):
            plen = nlri[i]; nb = (plen + 7) // 8
            addr = nlri[i + 1:i + 1 + nb] + bytes(4 - nb)
            comps.append((t, ipaddress.ip_network(f"{ipaddress.IPv4Address(addr)}/{plen}")))
            i += 1 + nb
            continue
        terms = []
        while True:
            op = nlri[i]; size = 1 &lt;&lt; ((op &gt;&gt; 4) & 3)
            val = int.from_bytes(nlri[i + 1:i + 1 + size], "big"); i += 1 + size
            terms.append((bool(op & 0x40) and bool(terms), op & 0x0f, val))
            if op & 0x80:
                break
        comps.append((t, terms))
    return comps

def term_str(t, bits, val):
    if t in BITMASK:
        flag = {0: "any", 1: "match", 2: "!any", 3: "!match"}[bits & 3]
        if t == 12:
            val = "+".join(k for k, v in FRAG.items() if val & v)
        return f"{flag} {val}"
    return {v: k for k, v in OPS.items()}.get(bits & 7, "?") + str(val)

def show(comps):
    parts = []
    for t, a in comps:
        if t in (1, 2):
            parts.append(f"{NAMES[t]} {a}")
        else:
            s = ""
            for k, (and_bit, bits, val) in enumerate(a):
                s += ("" if k == 0 else " && " if and_bit else " || ") + term_str(t, bits, val)
            parts.append(f"{NAMES[t]} {{{s}}}")
    return " AND ".join(parts)

def eval_terms(t, terms, x):
    """AND binds tighter than OR (RFC 8955 4.2.1.1)."""
    result, group = False, None
    for and_bit, bits, val in terms:
        if t in BITMASK:
            hit = (x & val) == val if bits & 1 else (x & val) != 0
            hit = not hit if bits & 2 else hit
        else:
            hit = (bits & 4 and x &lt; val) or (bits & 2 and x &gt; val) or (bits & 1 and x == val)
        if and_bit:
            group = group and hit
        else:
            result = result or bool(group)
            group = hit
    return result or bool(group)

def matches(comps, p):
    for t, a in comps:
        if t == 1 and ipaddress.ip_address(p["dst"]) not in a: return False
        if t == 2 and ipaddress.ip_address(p["src"]) not in a: return False
        if t == 3 and not eval_terms(3, a, p["proto"]): return False
        if t in (4, 5, 6):
            if p["proto"] not in (6, 17) or p.get("frag_off", 0):    # no ports in later fragments
                return False
            ports = {4: (p["sport"], p["dport"]), 5: (p["dport"],), 6: (p["sport"],)}[t]
            if not any(eval_terms(t, a, x) for x in ports): return False
        if t == 10 and not eval_terms(10, a, p["len"]): return False
        if t == 12:
            f = (1 if p.get("df") else 0) | (2 if p.get("frag_off", 0) else 0) \
                | (4 if not p.get("frag_off", 0) and p.get("mf") else 0) \
                | (8 if p.get("frag_off", 0) and not p.get("mf") else 0)
            if not eval_terms(12, a, f): return False
    return True

def traffic_rate(asn, rate_bytes_per_s):
    """Transitive extended community 0x8006: 2-octet id + IEEE 754 float (bytes/s)."""
    return struct.pack("!BBHf", 0x80, 0x06, asn & 0xFFFF, rate_bytes_per_s)

