IANA’s trust anchor file (abridged to the fields used here; fetched with curl -sS https://data.iana.org/root-anchors/root-anchors.xml):
&lt;KeyDigest id="Kjqmt7v" validFrom="2010-07-15T00:00:00+00:00" validUntil="2019-01-11T00:00:00+00:00"&gt;
    &lt;KeyTag&gt;19036&lt;/KeyTag&gt;
    &lt;Digest&gt;49AAC11D7B6F6446702E54A1607371607A1A41855200FD2CE1CDDE32F24E8FB5&lt;/Digest&gt;
&lt;KeyDigest id="Klajeyz" validFrom="2017-02-02T00:00:00+00:00"&gt;
    &lt;KeyTag&gt;20326&lt;/KeyTag&gt;
    &lt;Digest&gt;E06D44B80B8F1D39A95C0B0D7C65D08458E880409BBC683457104237C7F8EC8D&lt;/Digest&gt;
&lt;KeyDigest id="Kmyv6jo" validFrom="2024-07-18T00:00:00+00:00"&gt;
    &lt;KeyTag&gt;38696&lt;/KeyTag&gt;
    &lt;Digest&gt;683D2D0ACB8C9B712A1948B27F741219298D0A450D612C483AF444A4C0FB2B16&lt;/Digest&gt;
KSK-2010 (19036) has a validUntil; the other two do not. The box this was written on cannot send DNS queries straight to the root servers (port 53 to the Internet is filtered), so rootkeys.py asks for the root DNSKEY set over DNS-over-HTTPS (RFC 8484) with dnspython 2.9.1, then recomputes each KSK’s DS digest:
import urllib.request
import dns.message, dns.query, dns.rdatatype, dns.dnssec, dns.name, datetime
q = dns.message.make_query('.', 'DNSKEY', want_dnssec=True, payload=4096)
req = urllib.request.Request('https://cloudflare-dns.com/dns-query', data=q.to_wire(),
                             headers={'content-type': 'application/dns-message', 'accept': 'application/dns-message'})
r = dns.message.from_wire(urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=10).read())   # RFC 8484 DoH POST
keys = [rr for rr in r.answer if rr.rdtype == dns.rdatatype.DNSKEY][0]
sigs = [rr for rr in r.answer if rr.rdtype == dns.rdatatype.RRSIG][0]
print(f"DNSKEY RRset for '.', TTL {keys.ttl}, via DNS-over-HTTPS (cloudflare-dns.com)")
for k in sorted(keys, key=lambda k: -k.flags):
    role = {257: 'KSK (SEP)', 256: 'ZSK'}.get(k.flags & 0xFFFF & ~0x80, str(k.flags))
    rev = ' REVOKED' if k.flags & 0x80 else ''
    print(f"  flags={k.flags:&lt;3} alg={k.algorithm.value} tag={dns.dnssec.key_id(k):&lt;5} {role}{rev}")
for s in sigs:
    exp = datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp(s.expiration, datetime.UTC)
    print(f"RRSIG over DNSKEY: signed by key tag {s.key_tag}, expires {exp:%Y-%m-%d %H:%M} UTC")
for tag, digest in ((20326, 'E06D44B80B8F1D39A95C0B0D7C65D08458E880409BBC683457104237C7F8EC8D'),
                    (38696, '683D2D0ACB8C9B712A1948B27F741219298D0A450D612C483AF444A4C0FB2B16')):
    for k in keys:
        if dns.dnssec.key_id(k) == tag:
            ds = dns.dnssec.make_ds(dns.name.root, k, 'SHA256')
            print(f"tag {tag}: DS digest matches IANA root-anchors.xml: {ds.digest.hex().upper() == digest}")
$ python rootkeys.py
DNSKEY RRset for '.', TTL 2562, via DNS-over-HTTPS (cloudflare-dns.com)
  flags=257 alg=8 tag=20326 KSK (SEP)
  flags=257 alg=8 tag=38696 KSK (SEP)
  flags=256 alg=8 tag=8763  ZSK
RRSIG over DNSKEY: signed by key tag 38696, expires 2026-11-11 00:00 UTC
tag 20326: DS digest matches IANA root-anchors.xml: True
tag 38696: DS digest matches IANA root-anchors.xml: True
Both KSKs are still published, but the only RRSIG over the DNSKEY set comes from 38696. The TTL is what was left in Cloudflare’s cache; the root zone itself publishes DNSKEY with a 172800-second (two-day) TTL. Both digests match IANA’s file byte for byte, which is exactly the check unbound-anchor does (plus verifying the S/MIME signature in root-anchors.p7s).

Deploying it

Behind a CDN (Cloudflare and others): ECH is a zone setting. The CDN runs the client-facing servers, rotates keys and publishes the HTTPS records. Lab 1 shows the result.
Your own server: you need a TLS stack with server-side ECH, a public name with a certificate, and automation that publishes each new ECHConfig in DNS before the matching private key goes live, then removes the old one after its TTL has passed. Caddy 2.10 added this as a built-in feature. Its ech global option names the public name(s) and its dns global option gives it a DNS provider module to publish the HTTPS record. Written from the Caddy documentation, not run here (it needs a DNS-provider module, API credentials and a public domain):

{
    dns cloudflare {env.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN}
    ech ech.example.net
}

private.example.net {
    respond "hello from behind ECH"
}
Caddy then generates the ECH keys, gets a certificate for ech.example.net, and publishes ech in the HTTPS record of private.example.net. Check the outcome with the Lab 1 script against your own name.

OpenSSL 4.0 based servers: OpenSSL 4.0 (April 2026) provides the ECH API, so servers built on it can add support. Check your server’s own documentation for whether and how it exposes ECH.

Deploying MTA-STS
Deploying MTA-STS on the receiving side requires setting up policy hosting, issuing Web PKI certificates, and configuring DNS records in a deliberate sequence.

1. Web Server Configuration (Nginx)
The policy file must be accessible via HTTPS at https://mta-sts.&lt;domain&gt;/.well-known/mta-sts.txt.
nginx configuration (not run here). default_type sets the Content-Type for a file without a known extension mapping; do not also add a Content-Type header with add_header, or the response carries two:


mta-sts-nginx.conf

server {
    listen 443 ssl;
    server_name mta-sts.example.com;

    ssl_certificate /etc/letsencrypt/live/mta-sts.example.com/fullchain.pem;
    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/mta-sts.example.com/privkey.pem;

    location = /.well-known/mta-sts.txt {
        default_type text/plain;
        alias /var/www/mta-sts/mta-sts.txt;
    }

    location / {
        return 404;
    }
}



2. Policy File Creation
Create /var/www/mta-sts/mta-sts.txt:
version: STSv1
mode: testing
mx: mail.example.com
mx: *.backup.example.com
max_age: 86400


3. DNS Records
Publish the initial DNS records:
_mta-sts.example.com.   IN TXT "v=STSv1; id=20261114T01;"
_smtp._tls.example.com. IN TXT "v=TLSRPTv1; rua=mailto:tlsrpt@example.com;"


4. Operational Update Order (RFC 8461 Section 8.1)
Section 8.1 says to update the HTTPS policy body before the TXT record, so a sender that sees the new id cannot fetch and cache the old body under it. In practice:

Adding an MX Host: First, add the new MX hostname to the HTTPS policy file. Second, change the TXT record id so senders refetch. Third, wait at least the TXT record’s TTL, and then change the MX records. Senders that recheck the TXT record only when delivery fails (section 5.1) will pick up the new policy; senders that cached the old policy without rechecking defer until they do (day 2.0 in the lab).
Changing Mode to Enforce: Start in mode: testing with a low max_age (e.g., 86400) alongside TLSRPT monitoring. After reviewing reports and confirming zero certificate or mismatch errors, change mode to enforce in the HTTPS policy file, increment the TXT record id, and gradually scale max_age up to 1209600 (14 days) or 2419200 (28 days).
Decommissioning MTA-STS (Section 8.3): Publish a policy with mode: none and a small max_age (for example one day), change the TXT id, and keep both until every previously served policy has expired (last time served plus its max_age, and older policies may have had a longer one). Then remove the TXT record and the HTTPS endpoint.
Delegation via CNAME (Section 8.2): Organizations with many domains can delegate policy discovery using CNAME records:
_mta-sts.customer.example. IN CNAME _mta-sts.managedprovider.example.
However, the HTTPS policy host (mta-sts.customer.example) must still serve a Web PKI certificate valid for mta-sts.customer.example.

